	affiliations = {Computer Science Department, Universidade da Beira Interior, Covilhã, Portugal},
	abstract = {The rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLMs) is reshaping the software engineering landscape, powering AI-driven tools that assist in coding, testing, documentation, and deployment. As these systems evolve toward greater autonomy, the field of AI for Software Engineering (AI4SE) faces a pivotal question: how much responsibility should be entrusted to machines within complex engineering workflows? This position paper examines the potential and limitations of LLMs as autonomous actors in software development. Drawing on state-of-the-art tools, emerging agentic prototypes, and insights from cutting-edge literature, we argue that autonomy in software engineering should be conceptualized as a continuum—one that requires human-centered design, rigorous evaluation, and explainability. We identify six key barriers to full autonomy, spanning technological, human, organizational, evaluative, and environmental dimensions, and propose a framework for responsible autonomy grounded in hybrid intelligence, traceability, and institutional preparedness. Ultimately, we advocate for intentional, interdisciplinary strategies to guide the future of AI4SE. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s)},
	author_keywords = {Agentic AI; AI4SE; Large Language Models; Software Engineering Automation},
