	abstract = {Decentralised Applications (DApps) have revolutionised data traceability and accountability through smart contracts. However, code-level vulnerabilities in smart contracts remain a critical threat to these networks. Traditional detection methods often rely on isolated syntax-level source code analysis or on a predefined set of rules. Hence, they often fail to capture intricate details and complex behavioural semantics. Recently, Large Language Models (LLMs) have emerged as a promising alternative, offering deeper semantic insights and humaninterpretable reasoning. This study investigates the efficacy of the two LLMs, DeepSeek-Coder and Qwen-Coder, for identifying smart contract vulnerabilities. Findings demonstrate that while specialised classifiers may offer high accuracy, parameter-rich LLMs provide superior explainability, offering a scalable path toward secure smart contract development. © 2026 IEEE.},
